    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-9636",
      "problem": "What is the graph of $y = f(x),$ where $f(x) = 2x - 3?$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that the graph of $y = 2x - 3$ is a straight line, and two distinct points can determine any straight line.\n\nSo, given any two distinct values of $x,$ we can find the corresponding $y$-values by substituting $x$ into the formula $y = 2x - 3.$\nFor example,\n\n- if $x = 0$ then $y = 2(0) - 3 = -3,$ which gives the point $(0,-3),$ and\n\n- if $x = 1$ then $y = 2(1) - 3 = -1,$ which gives the point $(1,-1).$\n\nFinally, we plot these $(x,y)$ pairs in the $xy$-plane and draw a line through them. This gives the following graph.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14189776/assets/9f3005c9fac13668b126396f11aa8f3141d6de63d6e88e90d270901a9253fbd1.png)",
